From dc7d95731a7284e0bb4b6942582b8e7d797e25fa Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Sergey Fedorov <barracuda@macos-powerpc.org>
Date: Fri, 23 Oct 2026 04:18:44 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 17/20] feat(ppc): AltiVec kernels for G4/G5 and other pre-VSX
 PowerPC

The PowerPC kernels in arch/ppc64 required VSX (POWER7+), so G4/G5 and
POWER6 builds, and every 32-bit PowerPC build, fell back to the scalar
kernels. Apart from unaligned loads/stores (vec_xl/vec_xst), float
division, one vec_mul and one integer vec_cmpge, the kernels already
used only AltiVec (VMX) operations.

- arch/ppc64/ppc_simd.h wraps the differing operations: ppc_load and
  ppc_store (VSX forms, or the classic lvsl/vperm load and an aligned
  stvx / plain-copy store on AltiVec) and ppc_div (vdivsp, or a refined
  vrefp estimate). The kernels and the KV-cache fp16 conversion use the
  wrappers; vec_mul and vec_cmpge are replaced by AltiVec equivalents.
- Kernel code is guarded by USE_ALTIVEC; USE_VSX implies it. The arch
  is renamed PPC (-DFORGE_ARCH=PPC64 still works) and also covers 32-bit
  PowerPC and Darwin's "Power Macintosh".
- detect_cpu_features: probe __VSX__/__ALTIVEC__ with the CPU flags the
  build uses (FORGE_TUNE_MARCH, or -mcpu=native for native builds);
  -DFORGE_CPU_FEATURES=ALTIVEC|VSX overrides. Without an AltiVec CPU in
  the flags the scalar kernels are kept, so default 32-bit builds stay
  G3-safe. AltiVec builds add -maltivec (and -mabi=altivec off Darwin);
  VSX builds add -mcpu=power8 as before unless FORGE_TUNE_MARCH is set.
- Cross tests: AltiVec variants of the PowerPC kernel tests, built for a
  7450 and run under qemu-ppc -cpu 7450 (which traps on VSX), with the
  compiler and extra flags configurable. Add a ppc_div accuracy test.

On a 32-bit big-endian 7450 (qemu-ppc) the PowerPC kernel suite (342
checks) and the IQ dot/GEMV tests pass. The kernel sources compile for
both -mcpu=7450 and -mcpu=power7 (VSX).

